Ladybird fetches a CSS image when layout builds the box of an element
whose computed style uses it, and it updates layout before the load
event so that those fetches delay it. The other engines do the same.
Blink loads an element’s images after the cascade, skipping elements
whose display is none, in StyleResolverState::LoadPendingResources(),
and its Document::FinishedParsing() calls UpdateStyleAndLayoutTree().
Gecko starts them from nsIFrame::DidSetComputedStyle(), through
ComputedStyle::StartImageLoads(), so only for elements that get a frame.
And WebKit loads them through Style::loadPendingResources() as a
RenderElement takes its style, after its Document::finishedParsing()
has called updateStyleIfNeeded().
